From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herrie <github@herrie.org>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] lxc: do not claim NFC the container cannot reach

setup_host_perms() copies the host's NFC feature declarations into the
container, and a declaration is a promise: Android reads it, decides the
hardware is present, and starts the stack that drives it. NfcService comes
up, libnfc-nci sends an NCI command, nothing on the other end answers, and
nfc_ncif_cmd_timeout() aborts com.android.nfc. That process is persistent,
so Android restarts it, and it aborts again - measured on sargo at twelve
crashes in seven minutes, from boot onwards, indefinitely.

The cost is not confined to NFC. A persistent process failing on a loop
keeps system_server busy restarting it, and the platform service goes with
it: waydroid.active_apps and sys.boot_completed start answering empty,
"waydroid app launch" reports success and produces no window, and app
launches that normally take a fraction of a second take many seconds or
never finish. It reads as Waydroid being slow and losing apps, which is how
this was found.

Copying the declaration is right on a host that hands its NFC HAL to the
container, which is what upstream assumes. LuneOS is not that host: the
controller belongs to the host side, where webos-nfc-adapter drives it, and
nothing bridges an INfc into the container - nor could a second stack have
the controller if it did. So do not claim the feature. It is the same call
already made for telephony with ro.radio.noril: the honest answer to "is
this reachable from in here" is no, and Android copes with absent hardware
far better than with hardware that never replies.

This is reachable here in a way it is not upstream because 0001 dropped the
Treble check, so the copy now runs on Halium hosts too. consumerir is left
as it is: nothing on the host claims the IR blaster.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS runs its own NFC stack on the host]
---
diff --git a/tools/helpers/lxc.py b/tools/helpers/lxc.py
index 49ceb40..220ed5c 100644
--- a/tools/helpers/lxc.py
+++ b/tools/helpers/lxc.py
@@ -362,17 +362,28 @@ def setup_host_perms(args):
 
     sku = tools.helpers.props.host_get(args, "ro.boot.product.hardware.sku")
     copy_list = []
-    copy_list.extend(
-        glob.glob("/vendor/etc/permissions/android.hardware.nfc.*"))
+    # No NFC. A feature declaration copied in here tells Android the hardware
+    # is there, and Android then starts the stack that talks to it: NfcService
+    # comes up, libnfc-nci sends an NCI command, nothing answers, and
+    # nfc_ncif_cmd_timeout() aborts com.android.nfc. It is a persistent
+    # process, so Android restarts it and it aborts again - measured on sargo
+    # at twelve crashes in seven minutes, from boot, forever.
+    #
+    # The usual Waydroid host hands its NFC HAL to the container, so copying
+    # the declaration is right there. LuneOS never does: the NFC controller
+    # belongs to the host, where webos-nfc-adapter drives it, and a second
+    # stack inside the container could not have it even if one were bridged.
+    # So do not claim the feature. This is the same call as ro.radio.noril for
+    # telephony - the honest answer to "is this hardware reachable from in
+    # here" is no.
+    #
+    # consumerir is left alone: nothing on the host claims the IR blaster, so
+    # the declaration is what stops ConsumerIrService looking for a HAL.
     if os.path.exists("/vendor/etc/permissions/android.hardware.consumerir.xml"):
         copy_list.append("/vendor/etc/permissions/android.hardware.consumerir.xml")
-    copy_list.extend(
-        glob.glob("/odm/etc/permissions/android.hardware.nfc.*"))
     if os.path.exists("/odm/etc/permissions/android.hardware.consumerir.xml"):
         copy_list.append("/odm/etc/permissions/android.hardware.consumerir.xml")
     if sku != "":
-        copy_list.extend(
-            glob.glob("/odm/etc/permissions/sku_{}/android.hardware.nfc.*".format(sku)))
         if os.path.exists("/odm/etc/permissions/sku_{}/android.hardware.consumerir.xml".format(sku)):
             copy_list.append(
                 "/odm/etc/permissions/sku_{}/android.hardware.consumerir.xml".format(sku))
